<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-06">
  <title>Week 6: Convergence of Measurable Functions (2E) </title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Littlewood">
    <title>Littlewood's Three Principles</title>

    <p>
        Recall the definitions of pointwise and uniform convergence.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-pointwise-convergence">
        <title>Pointwise and Uniform Convergence</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>X</m> is a set, <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> are functions from <m>X</m> to <m>\mathbf{R}</m> and <m>f</m> is a function from <m>X</m> to <m>\mathbf{R}</m>. 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The sequence <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots </m> converges pointwise on <m>X</m> to <m>f</m> if 
                        <md>
                            \lim_{k\to \infty} f_k(x) = f(x)
                        </md>
                        for each <m>x \in X</m>. In other words, <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> converges pointwise on <m>X</m> to <m>f</m> if for each <m>x \in X</m> and every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists <m>n \in \mathbf{N}</m> such that <m>|f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \epsilon</m> for all integers <m>k \geq n</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The sequence <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> converges uniformly on <m>X</m> to <m>f</m> if for every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists <m>n \in \mathbf{N}</m> such that <m>|f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \epsilon</m> for all integers <m>k \geq n</m> and all <m>x \in X</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Example: Let <m>g_n(x) = x^n</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>. Then <m>g(x)</m> converges pointwise to 
        <md>
            g(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill 0 \amp \text{ if $ 0 \leq x &lt; 1$} \\
      \hfill  1 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x = 1$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>
        However, <m>g_n(x)</m> does not converge uniformly on <m>[0,1]</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The previous example shows that the pointwise limit of a sequence of continuous functions need not be continuous; however, the uniform limit of a sequence of continuous functions will always be continuous.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-unfiform-limit-continuous">
        <title>Uniform Limit of a Sequence of Continuous Function is Continuous</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>B \subseteq \mathbf{R}</m> and <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> is a sequence of functions from <m>B</m> to <m>\mathbf{R}</m>. Suppose <m>b \in B</m> and <m>f_k</m> is continuous at <m>b</m> for each <m>k \in \mathbf{R}</m>. Then <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>B</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        The following propositions are clearly false.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measurable set is a finite union of intervals.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measurable function is continuous.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every pointwise convergent sequence is uniformly convergent.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        However, it only takes a slight modification to change them into true statements. These are called <term>Littlewood's Three Principles</term>. The following are true statements.
    </p>

    <ol marker="(a)">
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measureable set is <em>nearly</em> a finite union of intervals.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every measurable function is <em>nearly</em> continuous.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Every pointwise convergent sequence is <em>nearly</em> uniformly convergent.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    
    <p>
        Here <em>nearly</em> means that the statement holds outside a set with arbitrarily small measure.
    </p>
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Egorov">
    <title>Egorov's Theorem</title>

    <p>
        We essentially proved (a) in our theorem of equivalences of being a Lebesgue measurable set. Next we will prove (c).
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-Egorov">
        <title>Egorov's Theorem</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>(X,S,\mu)</m> is a measure space with <m>\mu(X) &lt; \infty</m>. Suppose <m>f_1,f_2, \ldots</m> is a sequence of <m>S</m>-measurable functions from <m>X</m> to <m>\mathbf{R}</m> that converges pointwise on <m>X</m> to a function <m>f:X \to \mathbf{R}</m>. Then for every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists a set <m>E \in S</m> such that <m>\mu(X \setminus E) &lt; \epsilon</m> and <m>f_1,f_2,\ldots</m> converges uniformly to <m>f</m> on <m>X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        Example: Consider the sequence <m>g_n(x) = x^n</m>, which is a sequence of Lebesgue measurable functions from <m>X = [0,1]</m> to <m>\mathbf{R}</m> which converges pointwise to
        <md>
            g(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill 0 \amp \text{ if $ 0 \leq x &lt; 1$} \\
      \hfill  1 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x = 1$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>
        
    </p>

    <p>
        Given <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>, let <m>E = [0, 1-\frac{\epsilon}{2}]</m>. Then <m>\mu(X \setminus E) = \frac{\epsilon}{2} &lt; \epsilon</m> and <m>g_n(x)</m> converges uniformly to <m>g(x)</m> on <m>E</m>.
    </p>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> and temporarily fix <m>n \in \mathbf{N}</m>. Because <m>\{f_n\}</m> converges pointwise to <m>f</m>, we have that
            <md>
                \bigcup_{m=1}^{\infty} \bigcap_{k=m}^{\infty} \left\{x\in X: |f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt;  \frac{1}{n}\right\} = X
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
           For <m>m \in \mathbf{N}</m> let 
           <md>
            A_{m,n} = \bigcap_{k=m}^{\infty} \left\{x\in X: |f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \frac{1}{n}\right\}.
           </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Because each <m>f_k</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function <m>f</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function. Hence each <m>f_k-f</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function and <m>A_{m,n} \in S</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>A_{1,n} \subseteq A_{2,n} \subseteq \ldots</m> is an increasing sequence of sets and 
            <md>
                \bigcup_{m=1}^{\infty} A_{m,n} = X
            </md>
            it follows that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{m\to \infty} \mu(A_{m,n}) = \mu(X)</m>.
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus there exists <m>m_n \in \mathbf{N}</m> such that <m>\mu(X) - \mu(A_{m_n,n}) &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2^n}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now let <m>E = \displaystyle \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_{m_n,n}</m>. Then
            <md>
                <mrow> \mu(X \setminus E) \amp \left( X \setminus \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_{m_n,n} \right) </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \mu \left( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} X \setminus A_{m_n,n} \right)</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp \leq \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(X \setminus A_{m_n,n})</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp \leq \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \mu(X) - \mu(A_{m_n,n}) = \epsilon. </mrow>
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            To complete the proof, we need to show that the sequence <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> converges to <m>f</m> uniformly on <m>E</m>. Suppose <m>\epsilon' &gt; 0</m> and let <m>n \in \mathbf{N}</m> be such that <m>\frac{1}{n} &lt; \epsilon'</m>. Then because <m>E \subseteq A_{m_n,n}</m> we have that
            <md>
                |f_k(x)-f(x)| &lt; \frac{1}{n} &lt; \epsilon'
            </md>
             for all <m>k \geq m_n</m> and <m>x \in E</m>. Hence <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> converges uniformly to <m>f</m> on <m>E</m>.
        </p>

    </proof>
    
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-simple-functions">
    <title>Approximation by Simple Functions</title>
    

    <p>
        The simplest function is a constant function, since it only attains a single value. The next simplest are functions that only attain a finite number of values.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-simple-function">
        <title>Simple Function</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                A function is called <term>simple</term> if it takes on only a finite number of values.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Example: Dirichlet's function
        <md>
            f(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  1 \amp \text{ if $x \in \mathbf{Q}$} \\
      \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x \notin \mathbf{Q}$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>

        is a simple function since it only takes on 2 values.
        
    </p>

    <p>
        Suppose <m>(X,S)</m> is a measurable space, <m>f: X \to \mathbf{R}</m> is a simple function and <m>c_1, c_2, \ldots c_n</m> are the distinct nonzero values of <m>f</m>. Then <m>f = c_1 \chi_{E_1} + c_2 \chi_{E_2} + \ldots + c_n \chi_{E_n}</m> where <m>E_k = f^{-1}(c_k)</m>. This function is an <m>S</m>-measurable function if and only if all <m>E_k \in S</m>. 
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-approximation-simple-functions">
        <title>Approximation by Simple Functions</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>(X,S)</m> is a measurable space and <m>f:X \to [-\infty, \infty]</m> is <m>S</m>-measurable. Then there exists a sequence <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots,</m> of functions from <m>X</m> to <m>\mathbf{R}</m> such that
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Each <m>f_k</m> is a simple <m>S</m>-measurable function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>|f_k(x)| \leq |f_{k+1}(x)| \leq |f(x)|</m> for all <m>k \in \mathbf{N}</m> and <m>x \in X</m>. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{k\to \infty} f_k(x) = f(x)</m> for every <m>x \in X</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> converges uniformly to <m>f</m> if <m>f</m> is bounded.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Define <m>f_k(x)</m> by 
            <md>
                f_k(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  \frac{m}{2^k} \amp \text{ if $0 \leq f(x) \leq k$ and $m \in \mathbf{Z}$ is such that $f(x) \in [\frac{m}{2^k}, \frac{m+1}{2^k})$} \\
      \hfill  \frac{m+1}{2^k} \amp \text{ if $-k \leq f(x) &lt; 0$ and $m \in \mathbf{Z}$ is such that $f(x) \in [\frac{m}{2^k}, \frac{m+1}{2^k})$} \\
      \hfill k \amp \text{ if $f(x) &gt; k$} \\
      \hfill -k \amp \text{ if $f(x) &lt; -k$} \\
  \end{cases}
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            In other words, we divide <m>[-k,k]</m> into equally spaced subintervals. If <m>f(x) \in [0,k]</m> we define <m>f_k(x)</m> to be the left endpoint of the subinterval it's in and if <m>f(x) \in [-k,0)</m> we define <m>f_k(x)</m> to be the right endpoint of the subintervals it's in. If <m>|f(x)| &gt; k</m> then we define <m>f_k(x) = \pm k</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            For each <m>k</m>, <m>f_k(x)</m> only attains a finite number of values, so it is a simple function. Moreover, because <m>f</m> is an <m>S</m>-measurable function <m>f^{-1}\left([\frac{m}{2^k}, \frac{m+1}{2^k})\right) \in S</m>. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus for each <m>y \in \mathbf{R}</m> <m>f_k^{-1}(y)</m> is a Borel set and thus <m>f_k</m> is also an <m>S</m>-measurable function, proving (a). Also (b) holds because of how we define <m>f_k(x)</m>. For example if <m>k &lt; j</m> and <m>f(x)</m> is in <m> [\frac{m}{2^k}, \frac{m+1}{2^k})</m> and also in <m>[\frac{p}{2^j}, \frac{p+1}{2^j})</m>, then <m>\frac{m}{2^k} &lt; \frac{p}{2^j}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            It follows from how we defined <m>f_k</m> that
            <md>
                |f_k(x)-f(x)| \leq \frac{1}{2^k} 
            </md>
            for all <m>x</m> such that <m>f(x) \in [-k,k]</m>. Hence for each <m>x \in X</m>, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{k\to \infty} f_k(x) = f(x)</m>. Thus (c) is proved.
        </p>

        <p>
            Finally if <m>f</m> is bounded then there exists some <m>K</m> such that for all <m>x</m> <m>f(x) \in [-K,K]</m> and thus the previous limit gives that <m>\{f_k\}</m> converges uniformly to <m>f</m>, proving (d).
        </p>

    </proof>
    


  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Luzin">
    <title>Luzin's Theorem</title>

    <p>
        Now we will prove the second of Littlewood's Three Principles. 
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-luzin">
        <title>Luzin's Theorem</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>g: \mathbf{R} \to \mathbf{R}</m> is a Borel Measurable function. Then for every <m>\epsilon>0</m> there exists a closed set <m>F \subseteq \mathbf{R}</m> such that <m>|\mathbf{R} \setminus F| &lt; \epsilon</m> and <m>g|_F</m> is a continuous function on <m>F</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        Note that Luzin's Theorem does not say that <m>g</m> is continuous on <m>F</m>, only that <m>g|_F</m> is continuous on <m>F</m>. For example, Dirichlet's function
        <md>
            g(x) = \begin{cases} 
      \hfill  1 \amp \text{ if $x \in \mathbf{Q}$} \\
      \hfill  0 \hfill \amp \text{ if $x \notin \mathbf{Q}$} 
  \end{cases}
        </md>
        is nowhere continuous on <m>\mathbf{R}</m>. Let <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> and let <m>G</m> be an open cover of <m>\mathbf{Q}</m> with <m>|G| &lt; \epsilon</m>. Let <m>F = \mathbf{R} \setminus G</m>. Then <m>F</m> is a closed set and <m>|\mathbf{R} \setminus F| &lt; \epsilon</m>. Since <m>g(x) = 0</m> on <m>F</m>, <m>g|_F</m> is a continuous function on <m>F</m>. 
    </p>

    <proof>
        <p>
            We will build up the proof through a series of special cases. First consider the case that <m>g</m> is a simple function. Recall that this means that <m>g</m> can be written as
            <md>
                g = d_1 \chi_{D_1} + d_2 \chi_{D_2} + \ldots + d_n \chi_{D_n}
            </md>
            where <m>d_1, \ldots d_n</m> are distinct nonzero real numbers and <m>D_1, \ldots D_n</m> are disjoint Borel sets.
        </p>

        <p>
            Suppose <m>\epsilon>0</m>. Then for each <m>k \in \{1,2, \ldots n\}</m> there exists a closed set <m>F_k \subseteq D_k</m> and an open set <m>G_k \supseteq D_k</m> such that
            <md>
                |G_k \setminus D_k| &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2n} \text{ and } |D_k \setminus F_k| &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2n}.
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Because <m>G_k \setminus F_k = (G_k \setminus D_k) \cup (D_k \setminus F_k)</m>, we have that <m>|G_k \setminus F_k| &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2n} + \frac{\epsilon}{2n} = \frac{\epsilon}{n}</m>  for each <m>k \in \mathbf{N}</m>. Now let
            <md>
                 F = \left( \bigcup_{k=1}^n F_k \right) \cup \bigcap_{k=1}^n \left( \mathbf{R} \setminus G_k \right).
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>F</m> is a finite union of closed sets, <m>F</m> is closed. Moverover,
            <md>
                \mathbf{R} \setminus F \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^n G_k \setminus F_k
            </md>
            and hence <m>\displaystyle |\mathbf{R}\setminus F| \leq \sum_{k=1}^n \frac{\epsilon}{n} = \epsilon</m>. Thus it remains to show that <m>g|_F</m> is a continuous function on <m>F</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Because <m>F_k \subseteq D_k</m> it follows that <m>g(x) = d_k</m> on <m>F_k</m>. Thus <m>g</m> is continuous on <m>F_k</m> for all <m>k \in \{1, \ldots, n\}</m>. In addition, since
            <md>
                \bigcap_{k=1}^n \mathbf{R} \setminus G_k \subseteq \bigcap_{k=1}^n \mathbf{R} \setminus D_k
            </md>
            it follows that <m>g(x) = 0</m> on this set, and hence <m>g</m> is continuous here as well. Thus <m>g</m> is continuous on <m>F</m> and the theorem is proven for simple functions.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now suppose that <m>g</m> is an arbitrary Borel function. Then there is a sequence of simple Borel measurable functions <m>g_1, g_2, \ldots</m> that converges to <m>g</m>. Suppose <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>. Then by the special case we just proved, for each <m>k</m> there exists a closed set <m>C_k</m> such that <m>|\mathbf{R} \setminus C_k| &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2^{k+1}}</m> and <m>g_k|_{C_k}</m> is continuous on <m>C_k</m>.  
        </p>

        <p>
            Let
            <md>
                C = \bigcap_{k=1}^{\infty} C_k.
            </md>
            Then <m>C</m> is a closed set and <m>g_k|_C</m> is continuous on <m>C</m> for all <m>k</m>. Note that
            <md>
                \mathbf{R} \setminus C = \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} \mathbf{R} \setminus C_k.
            </md>
            Hence
            <md>
                |\mathbf{R}\setminus C| \leq \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} |\mathbf{R}\setminus C_k| &lt; \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \frac{\epsilon}{2^{k+1}} = \frac{\epsilon}{2}. 
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            For each <m>m \in \mathbf{Z}</m> the sequence <m>g_1|_{(m,m+1)}, g_2|_{(m,m+1)}, \ldots</m> converges pointwise on <m>(m,m+1)</m> to <m>g|_{(m,m+1)}</m>. Thus by Egorov's Theorem for each <m>m \in \mathbf{Z}</m> there is a Borel set <m>E_m \subseteq (m,m+1)</m> such that <m>g_1, g_2, \ldots</m> converges uniformly to <m>g</m> and
            <md>
                |(m, m+1) \setminus E_m| \leq \frac{\epsilon}{2^{|m|+3}}.
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Hence <m>g_1, g_2, \ldots</m> converges uniformly to <m>g</m> on <m>C \cap E_m</m> for each <m>m \in \mathbf{Z}</m>. Since each <m>g_k|_C</m> is continuous it follows that <m>g</m> is continuous on <m>C \cap E_m</m> for each <m>m \in \mathbf{Z}</m>. Thus <m>g|_D</m> is continuous, where
            <md>
                D = \bigcup_{m \in \mathbf{Z}} C \cap E_m.
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Now
            <md>
                \mathbf{R} \setminus D \subseteq \left(\mathbf{R}\setminus C\right) \cup \bigcup_{m \in \mathbf{Z}} \left((m,m+1) \setminus E_m) \right) \cup \mathbf{Z}.
            </md>
            Hence
            <md>
                <mrow> |\mathbf{R}\setminus D| \amp \leq |\mathbf{R}\setminus C| + \sum_{m\in \mathbf{Z}} |(m, m+1) \setminus E_m| + |\mathbf{Z}| </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp &lt; \frac{\epsilon}{2} + \sum_{m\in \mathbf{Z}}\frac{\epsilon}{2^{|m|+3}} + 0 </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \frac{\epsilon}{2} + \frac{\epsilon}{8} + 2\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{\epsilon}{2^{m+3}} </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp =\frac{\epsilon}{2} + \frac{\epsilon}{8} + \frac{\epsilon}{4} &lt; \epsilon. </mrow>
            </md>
        Hence <m>|\mathbf{R}\setminus D| &lt; \epsilon</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Since <m>D</m> is a Borel set, we can approximate it from below by closed sets. This means there exists a closed set <m>F</m> such that <m>F \subseteq D</m> and <m>|D \setminus F| &lt; \epsilon - |\mathbf{R} \setminus D|</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Hence
            <md>
                |\mathbf{R} \setminus F| = |(\mathbf{R} \setminus D) \cup (D \setminus F)| \leq |\mathbf{R}\setminus D| + |D \setminus F| = \epsilon.
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
            Thus <m>F</m> is a closed set with <m>|\mathbf{R} \setminus F| &lt; \epsilon</m>. Moreover, since a continuous function is still continuous when restricted to a smaller set, <m>g|_F</m> is continuous on <m>F</m>, which completes the proof.
        </p>

    </proof>

    <p>
        There is another version of Luzin's Theorem which is sometimes more useful.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-luzin-2">
        <title>Luzin's Theorem, Second Version</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>E \subseteq \mathbf{R}</m> and <m>g: E \to \mathbf{R}</m> is a Borel measurable function. Then for every <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there exists a closed set <m>F \subseteq E</m> and a continuous function <m>h: \mathbf{R} \to \mathbf{R}</m> such that <m>|E \setminus F| &lt; \epsilon</m> and <m>h|_F = g|_F</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        See the textbook for the proof
    </p>

    
    
    
    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Lebesgue-Measurable-Functions">
    <title>Lebesgue Measurable Functions</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-Lebesgue-measurable-function">
        <title>Lebesgue Measurable Function</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                 A function <m>f: A \to \mathbf{R}</m> is called <term>Lebesgue measurable</term> if <m>f^{-1}(B)</m> is a Lebesgue measurable set for every Borel set <m>B</m> in <m>\mathbf{R}</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Recall that a Lebesgue measurable set differs from a Borel set by a set with outer measure <m>0</m>. Likewise we expect that every Lebesgue measurable function is nearly a Borel measurable function.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-lebesgue-meas-funct">
        <title>Every Lebesgue Measurable Function is Almost Borel Measurable</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>f: \mathbf{R} \to \mathbf{R}</m> is a Lebesgue measurable function. Then there exists a Borel measurable function <m>g: \mathbf{R} \to \mathbf{R}</m> such that 
                <md>
                    |\{x \in \mathbf{R}: g(x) \neq f(x)\}| = 0.
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            There exists a sequence <m>f_1, f_2, \ldots</m> of Lebesgue measurable simple functions that converges pointwise to <m>f</m>. This means that for each <m>k \in \mathbf{N}</m>, there exists <m>c_1, \ldots, c_n \in \mathbf{R}</m> and disjoint Lebesgue measurable sets <m>A_1, \ldots A_n</m> such that
            <md>
                f_k = c_1 \chi_{A_1} + c_2 \chi_{A_2} + \ldots c_n \chi_{A_n}.
            </md>
             
        </p>

        <p>
            We now approximate these Lebesgue measurable sets by Borel sets. For each <m>j \in \{1, \ldots, n\}</m> there exists a Borel set <m>B_j</m> such that <m>|A_j \setminus B_j| = 0</m>. Let
            <md>
                g_k = c_1 \chi_{B_1} + c_2 \chi_{B_2} + \ldots c_n \chi_{B_n}.
            </md>
            Then <m>g_k</m> is a Borel measurable function and <m>|\{x \in \mathbf{R}: g_k(x) \neq f_k(x)\}| = 0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            We will show that the sequence <m>\{g_k\}</m> converges to the Borel measurable function that we want. If <m>x \notin \displaystyle \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} \{x \in \mathbf{R}: g_k(x) \neq f_k(x)\}</m>, then <m>g_k(x) = f_k(x)</m> for all <m>k \in \mathbf{N}</m> and hence <m>\lim_{k \to \infty} g_k(x) = f(x)</m>. 
        </p>

        <p>
            Let <m>\displaystyle E = \{x \in \mathbf{R}: \lim_{k\to \infty} g_k(x) \text{ exists in } \mathbf{R}\}</m>. It follows from an exercise in the book (#14 b in Section 2B) that <m>E</m> is a Borel subset of <m>\mathbf{R}</m>. Also,
            <md>
                \mathbf{R} \setminus E \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} \{x\in \mathbf{R}: g_k(x) \neq f_k(x)\}.
            </md>
            
        </p>

        <p>
           Since <m>\mathbf{R} \setminus E</m> is contained in a countable union of sets with measure <m>0</m>, it follows that <m>|\mathbf{R} \setminus E| = 0</m>. 
        </p>

        <p>
           Let <m>g(x) = \lim_{k\to \infty} (\chi_E g_k)(x)</m>. If <m>x \in E</m>, then the limit exists because of how we defined <m>E</m>, and if <m>x\notin E</m>, then <m>\chi_E = 0</m> and the limit is <m>0</m> and hence exists. So <m>g(x)</m> is well defined. Since the product of Borel measurable functions is a Borel measurable function it follows that for each <m>k \in \mathbf{N}</m>, <m>\chi_E g_k</m> is a Borel measurable function. Since the pointwise limit of a sequence of Borel measurable functions is Borel measurable, it follows that <m>g</m> is a Borel measurable function.
        </p>

        <p>
            Finally,
            <md>
                \{x\in \mathbf{R}: g(x) \neq f(x) \} \subseteq \bigcup_{k=1}^{\infty} \{x\in \mathbf{R}: g_k(x) \neq f_k(x) \}.
            </md>
            Thus <m>\{x\in \mathbf{R}: g(x) \neq f(x) \}</m> is contained in a countable union of sets with measure <m>0</m> and thus <m>|\{x\in \mathbf{R}: g(x) \neq f(x) \}| = 0</m>. Thus <m>g</m> is a Borel measurable function with <m>|\{x\in \mathbf{R}: g(x) \neq f(x) \}| = 0</m>, which completes the proof.
        </p>

    </proof>
    
    
  </subsection>


  </section>